Input: {"question": "Which educational institution did Károly Szakonyi attend?", "documents": [{"doc_id": "d1", "text": "Title: The Formative Years of Károly Szakonyi: A Hungarian Literary Icon\n\nIn the heart of Budapest, Hungary, a young Károly Szakonyi was laying the foundations for a remarkable literary career. Born in 1935, Szakonyi would go on to become one of Hungary's most celebrated writers and playwrights, leaving an indelible mark on the country's cultural landscape. But before he rose to fame, Szakonyi's educational journey began at Madách Imre High School, a prestigious institution in Budapest that played a pivotal role in shaping his artistic vision. As a teenager, Szakonyi walked through the gates of Madách Imre High School, surrounded by the imposing buildings and rich history of the institution. Founded in 1872, the school was named after Imre Madách, a renowned Hungarian poet and playwright, whose works had a profound impact on the country's literary heritage. It was here, amidst the academic rigor and cultural richness of Madách Imre High School, that Szakonyi's passion for writing began to take root. During his time at the school, Szakonyi was exposed to a wide range of literary influences, from the works of Hungarian masters like Sándor Márai and Dezső Kosztolányi to the classical plays of Shakespeare and Molière. The school's emphasis on language, literature, and drama helped Szakonyi develop his writing skills, and he soon began to experiment with his own creative voice. One of Szakonyi's earliest literary endeavors was a school newspaper, which he co-founded with his classmates. This humble publication became a platform for the young writer to express himself, sharing his thoughts on literature, politics, and social issues. The experience not only honed his writing skills but also instilled in him a sense of responsibility and discipline, essential qualities for a writer. Szakonyi's time at Madách Imre High School was also marked by his involvement in the school's drama club. Under the guidance of dedicated teachers, he participated in numerous productions, both on and off stage. This exposure to the world of theater helped Szakonyi develop his skills as a playwright, and he soon began to write his own plays, many of which would go on to be performed at the school. The evidence of Szakonyi's attendance at Madách Imre High School is well-documented."}, {"doc_id": "d2", "text": "As Károly Szakonyi sat at his worn wooden desk, surrounded by stacks of dog-eared books and scattered papers, he stared out the window at the grey Budapest sky. His mind wandered back to the days when the sea air filled his lungs, and the sound of gulls crying overhead was his constant companion. It was a lifetime ago, yet the memories remained as vivid as the characters he brought to life on the page. He began to scribble notes in the margin of his manuscript, the words flowing effortlessly as he recalled the lectures of Professor Nakamura at Tokyo University of Marine Science and Technology. The old man's passion for the ocean's secrets had been infectious, and Károly's own fascination with the mysteries beneath the waves had been ignited. He remembered the way the sunlight danced through the aquariums in the university's research facility, casting an ethereal glow over the bustling students. As he wrote, the characters in his story began to take shape. There was Emiko, the brilliant and introverted marine biologist, who had been his study partner and confidante during those long, sleepless nights in Tokyo. And then there was Captain József, the grizzled old sailor who had regaled them with tales of the open sea over steaming bowls of ramen in the university's cafeteria. Károly's thoughts were interrupted by the sound of his wife, Erzsébet, calling from the kitchen. \"Károly, dear, would you like some coffee?\" He smiled, his mind still half-lost in the world of his story. \"Yes, thank you, my love. And perhaps some of those lovely Japanese biscuits you brought back from the market?\" As he waited for Erzsébet to bring in the tray, Károly's gaze fell upon the small, framed photograph on his desk. It was a faded snapshot of himself and Emiko, taken on the deck of a research vessel in Tokyo Bay. They were both grinning from ear to ear, their faces wind-whipped and sun-kissed. He felt a pang of nostalgia, but it was tempered by the knowledge that those experiences had shaped him into the writer he was today. With a sense of renewed purpose, Károly turned back to his manuscript, the words flowing effortlessly as the characters of his story came alive once more."}], "fallible_prior": [], "prior_explanation": "I am uncertain about Károly Szakonyi's specific educational institutions as this is not common general knowledge. I cannot verify this without external sources.", "fallible_cards": [{"doc_id": "d1", "card_id": "d1:c0", "entity": "Károly Szakonyi", "scope": "educational institution attended", "claim": "Károly Szakonyi attended Madách Imre High School.", "answer": "Madách Imre High School", "quote": "But before he rose to fame, Szakonyi's educational journey began at Madách Imre High School, a prestigious institution in Budapest that played a pivotal role in shaping his artistic vision. As a teenager, Szakonyi walked through the gates of Madách Imre High School, surrounded by the imposing buildings and rich history of the institution. Founded in 1872, the school was named after Imre Madách, a renowned Hungarian poet and playwright, whose works had a profound impact on the country's literary heritage. It was here, amidst the academic rigor and cultural richness of Madách Imre High School, that Szakonyi's passion for writing began to take root. During his time at the school, Szakonyi was exposed to a wide range of literary influences, from the works of Hungarian masters like Sándor Márai and Dezső Kosztolányi to the classical plays of Shakespeare and Molière. The school's emphasis on language,